WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.381 --> 00:00:08.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:09.770
PROFESSOR: Fantastic.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.040
All right, so here we are
on this gorgeous day James.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.770
I hope you appreciate the weather.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:14.603
JAMES LEONARD: I do.

00:00:14.603 --> 00:00:17.010
PROFESSOR: And so let's
just start by you just

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.470
introducing yourself, a little
bit about your background,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.284
and how you came to be here today.

00:00:21.284 --> 00:00:22.950
JAMES LEONARD: My name is James Leonard.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.200
And I am an artist.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.720
And I live and work
in Brooklyn, New York,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.210
but I'm back up here in the greater
Boston area with my current art work,

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.010
the Tent of Casually
Observed Phenologies.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.630
Which is a, for lack
of a perfect term, it's

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.360
what I think people would call
a performance installation.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.610
And this is a piece that was begun
back in February of this year, 2015.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.970
When I was an artist in
residence at the Boston

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.520
Center for the Arts for
four months, as part

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.500
of their program of trying
to encourage and foster

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.060
the emergence of new artworks that
are socially engaged artworks.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.640
PROFESSOR: My original inspiration
for this whole prediction,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.060
prediction x course, now was
so that the public would really

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.330
understand what's behind
climate change simulations,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.490
and that they would take seriously
the work that's being done.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.990
And that they would think about
their future much more reflectively.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.440
And that they wouldn't kind of
dismiss these predictions as uncertain

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.510
when they hear the word uncertainty.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.650
And then what resonated so much with
me about your work and your project

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.850
is that you are also, I think,
trying to get the public

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.720
to think more about the future and
divination traditions and climate

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.440
change.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.590
And I'm just curious if you can
tell us again a little bit more

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:49.020
about how the creation of the
tent and the research that you

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.590
did for this project relates
to the climate change issue.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.540
JAMES LEONARD: The research for me has
really been a process of discovery.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:04.560
Like, and I'm coming at this very
much as an artist and a humanist.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.556
As opposed to a scientist or
an engineer, and saying aha,

00:02:08.556 --> 00:02:09.389
I have a clear path.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:11.910
And I may be actually giving
scientists, like putting them

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.710
too much on the, I
don't know, on the peg,

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.960
saying that you guys must know
what you're after when you start.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.040
Because maybe you guys
begin with questions too.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.160
Maybe that's it's
called research overall.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.260
And for me, the first
question that, as well as

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.045
working on another
piece of media that was,

00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:30.450
or another performance
piece where I was shaving

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.670
a boat away into a sea of shavings.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.290
And this was in part a response to the
Arctic melt of 2012, which was one of,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:42.090
it's the largest on record
that we've ever experienced.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.390
And trying to relate to my own sort of
visceral emotions about that sense of--

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.080
the impermanence that that showed.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.840
The massive scale of change.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.280
And it came to me sort of as a
dreamlike image, this guy shaving

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.972
a boat out from underneath himself.

00:02:56.972 --> 00:02:58.680
And so while I was
working on that, I was

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.860
realizing that I was working
a lot in my relationship

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.830
to the past and the present.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.630
And somewhere along the way I
was thinking about the future.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:13.339
And I think it started as a very
cynical and naive moment, where I said,

00:03:13.339 --> 00:03:15.630
well if we're not going to
listen to climate scientists

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.130
we might as well be
listening to fortune tellers.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.970
And at that point I had a very,
very different relationship

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.000
to divination than I do now.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.460
Fast forward a couple
of years, and I've spent

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.740
a lot of time actually
working with diviners

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.720
of a number of different traditions.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.180
Doing artworks about impermanence.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.840
Doing artworks about my relationship
to try to understand divination.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.110
And this side journey has
taken me into territory

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:44.940
where I'm now rethinking a lot
of my own original assumptions

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.960
about divination as something that--

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.060
I know I held what feels to me sometimes
in vernacular conversation with friends

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.550
and colleagues, seems to
be sort of a stereotype of,

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.030
well divination is protoscience.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.800
It's science that isn't quite right yet.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.216
And when you actually get
computers to tell you the answer

00:04:04.216 --> 00:04:06.750
and run simulations for you,
you don't need it anymore.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.270
Or when you have an accurate scientific
theory, you don't need it anymore.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:14.990
And instead I've started seeing
it as a cultural practice.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.769
Having now experienced
it both as a diviner.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:21.180
I'm giving readings inside of
the tent about climate change.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.509
And experiencing numerous readings
myself, receiving readings.

00:04:24.509 --> 00:04:26.800
PROFESSOR: And that was part
of your research process--

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.966
JAMES LEONARD: That was
part of my research process.

00:04:28.966 --> 00:04:30.334
Very much to try out.

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.250
Starting with the store
front fortune tellers,

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.970
and then going into more traditional
religious and cultural spaces

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.770
that took a long time to get into.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.670
They're fairly closed communities.

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:44.160
A reading is made up of several
things, several components.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.590
There's a diviner in
the querent, which is

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.190
the person who does the reading and
the person who asked the question.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:54.580
And the reading itself begins
usually with a question or a concern,

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:55.990
something that has been brought.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.990
And then there is
often a tool in between

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.000
that will be your method of divination.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.090
Sometimes that's an internal method
in some cases like the people

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.420
who are members of the spiritualist
church will conduct seances.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.780
And so their method of divination
is happening from visions and things

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:10.490
happening within their head.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.060
PROFESSOR: Yeah we talk about
that a lot in the course.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.210
About all the different kinds
of processes that people use.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.180
And that some people use
random processes in nature.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.570
Some people take the more
human spiritual approach.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:24.780
And some people use some kind of
official randomizing influence,

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.950
like a tarot deck--

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:26.820
JAMES LEONARD: Like a tarot deck

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.170
PROFESSOR: or stones.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.850
JAMES LEONARD: Or the I Ching Like
flipping quarters to do the I Ching

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.260
or casting the yarrow sticks.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:32.340
PROFESSOR: So did you try all of those?

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.131
JAMES LEONARD: I haven't
tried all of them.

00:05:34.131 --> 00:05:41.040
And there's several hundred existing
different methods of divination.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.300
Some of which have been sort of lost to
history, but there are echoes of them.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.620
There's Lore of them.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.900
And I've found that that's sort of
a side interest that I've developed.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.540
Is just sort of the flavor.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.550
There's something about it that--

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.950
Very quickly as I was
just even exploring,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.310
let's say, the Wikipedia entry
on all the methods of divination.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:03.840
It's so long and half the
time it reads like something

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.040
from the 1960s conceptual
performance art.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.350
That for me really moved me.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.390
Like divination by dripping wax.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.150
Divination by the tracks of beetles.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.446
Divination by laughter.

00:06:16.446 --> 00:06:19.320
But the thing that all of these
divinatory experiences that I've had,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.680
when they function well
have had in common,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.610
is that they create a space
that does a couple of things.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:30.430
It creates a space that opens
up the mind for decision making.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:32.430
It's not a substitute for freewill.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.610
That's one of the first mistakes
that I think a lot of modern people

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.520
who are unexperienced in
divination come to it with.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.240
PROFESSOR: You mean that it's not
a way to see a preordained future.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.300
JAMES LEONARD: It's also not a
way to get a yes or no answer.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.260
PROFESSOR: Right, and you know what I
have experienced by being in your tent

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.170
and talking with you,
is that there really

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:57.480
is this aspect of getting people
to think about their alternatives,

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.540
and about possible futures, and about
what they know about their own future.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:06.180
And you were telling me earlier
that part of the role of the diviner

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.430
is to get the querent to really talk
and think openly, and to be in a space

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:15.070
where they can really kind
of have this experience where

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.470
they're focused on their own future.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.890
And where they sort of can see their
world around them, but not really.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:25.447
And I think that the space that you
made definitely accomplishes that.

00:07:25.447 --> 00:07:28.530
JAMES LEONARD: And this leads us to
what the other main function that I've

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:32.400
seen in any of the successful
divinatory experiences that I've had is.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.280
Which is, we're all taking
in so much information.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:41.680
By the time you're 35, 40, 50 years old,
you have decades of life experience.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.400
If you're an active
participant in the information

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.010
age, and you've read hundreds
of thousands of words.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:55.680
You've experienced maybe
hundreds of academic theses

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.060
and takes on something, if
you're an expert in your field.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.850
And how do you put all
those thoughts in order.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.240
But what ends up happening, and I
believe we've used this term before,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.470
that divination can function almost like
as a comb for all of those thoughts.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.270
All that information
that you've taken in.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.769
It's a way to sort of, if you
think about the way a comb can

00:08:14.769 --> 00:08:18.330
move through hair, and lay it
into line and give it shape.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.570
It's a way to take these hundreds
of thousands of different ideas

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.580
and start finding the
natural shape within it.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.080
PROFESSOR: You know, there's a
story that David Carrasco who

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.260
is an expert on Mesoamerican
cultures told us.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.940
I don't know whether we had
heard this story already

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.270
when I met you the first time.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:34.590
But he talks about the gods
being annoyed with the humans.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.990
That the humans on Earth
were learning too much

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:42.380
about the future and about the
world, and that they took a sort of,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:43.890
a philosophical mirror.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.700
And that they breathed on the
mirror to kind of fog up the view

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:48.630
that the humans have.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:51.180
But that nature, around
the humans, still

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.880
has all these hints about the
future and what's going on.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.140
And that a lot of the divinatory
practices in Mesoamerica

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.780
have a lot to do with nature
and natural phenomena.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:05.010
And the idea is that they're
trying to see past this fog.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.160
And it's the same kind of
thing you're talking about.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.290
About the periphery about.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:09.930
That there's information there
that people just don't normally

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:11.460
access in their everyday lives.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:15.500
And they're sort of trying to find
a way to access that information.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.720
And you know you might think
that I, as a scientist, you know,

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.060
wouldn't want to think
about the world that way.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.589
But I think that there are
a lot of different ways

00:09:23.589 --> 00:09:24.630
to think about the world.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.910
And what you've taught me,
really in talking to you,

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.437
is that just like you
said, you know that you--

00:09:29.437 --> 00:09:31.520
We were talking about
climate change a minute ago,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.740
and you said, you know that you
thought, well we might as well just

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.240
listen to fortune tellers.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.640
And when I started
working on this course.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:43.380
I think that I was probably pretty
biased against this kind of, well

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.997
just sort of human reflection.

00:09:44.997 --> 00:09:47.580
And what can you learn about the
future from human reflection.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.270
And I've really come to see that I don't
think you can predict the climate 100

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:52.890
years from now by human reflection.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.620
But I think you can get people
to think about their future.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:56.730
And think about choices.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:00.390
And so I'm curious just to come
back for a minute to climate change,

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:04.740
how has the reaction been
to this tent and how do you

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.470
feel you're doing in
getting people to think

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:12.570
about the future and their own futures
with respect to the climate also?

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:15.930
JAMES LEONARD: Well just two
days ago we were exhibiting

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:18.900
the piece in the south end of Boston.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.380
And I was just finishing up a
reading and I overheard somebody

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:23.840
outside who was--

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.860
We have a little sign
that sits out in front

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.882
of the piece that lets people know sort
of the general house rules, you know.

00:10:29.882 --> 00:10:31.840
Sort of, please be
respectful if we're engaged.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.340
And this is what we're doing.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:34.881
And this is what the piece is called.

00:10:34.881 --> 00:10:38.190
So it sort of helps put
people in the right state,

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:41.580
and have some instruction
for how to approach it.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.730
And there was somebody
who just sort of out loud

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.810
said, why would I talk to a
diviner about climate change?

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.810
And then somebody else said, well why
don't you go ask him and find out.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.000
PROFESSOR: All right that's a
perfect way to put the question.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.760
JAMES LEONARD: The short of it is,
this gentleman walked away not fully

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.468
convinced that tarot
works, doesn't work.

00:10:58.468 --> 00:11:02.430
But instead that it made him look
at everything that he has already

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.140
taken in himself about climate change.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:07.390
Some of the grim prophecies, some of the
hopeful stuff, some of the stuff that

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.530
says don't look at it ignore it.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.500
And juggle it just differently
in his head for the afternoon.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.810
And it got him thinking about it.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:21.147
And he even remarked that,
sort of, as he left that while

00:11:21.147 --> 00:11:22.230
that this is a great tool.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.855
Whether he's going to use tarot or not.

00:11:23.855 --> 00:11:27.120
But like just even this way of looking
at this problem, to start digesting it.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.880
Because I think that's one
of the things that I find why

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.680
I want to make art about the problem.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.060
Is it's so huge.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.770
It's so overwhelming.

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:36.577
I don't know--

00:11:36.577 --> 00:11:38.160
I don't even know about my fellow man.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.410
Just for myself, how do I bite this off?

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.430
PROFESSOR: I know people who
will be part of the course later,

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.520
who do the climate change
simulations and who do climate policy

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:48.809
research and all of this.

00:11:48.809 --> 00:11:50.100
And they also feel overwhelmed.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.890
And I actually think
that what you're doing

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:59.490
is a very important complement to making
people sort of take the perspective.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:04.770
And look at the context of all of
the different kinds of predictions

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:05.670
about climate.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:09.510
Most of which are not good
if we don't do anything.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:13.200
But that giving them a sense
that there are choices,

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.970
and that they can think
about the choices.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.430
And maybe they can even learn
something about the choices

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:20.775
from these somewhat time
tested or ancient techniques.

00:12:20.775 --> 00:12:23.400
You know, from talking to a lot
of people about climate change,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.820
have you gotten a sense of
whether people respond better

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:31.230
to maybe a positive message
or a negative message?

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:35.570
And how are you thinking more
personally about it makes a difference?

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:39.100
JAMES LEONARD: I think it's actually the
fact that divination is a personalized

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:39.600
story.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.876
Since you are the querent and you are
starting the story off with a question.

00:12:43.876 --> 00:12:45.750
There's an instant level
of investment there.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.530
And it's tailored to you.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.730
And I think that engages
people differently.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.187
That personalize it.

00:12:52.187 --> 00:12:55.020
And sometimes people are actually
just even looking for affirmation.

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.978
But they don't want just
a pat on the shoulder.

00:12:56.978 --> 00:12:58.630
They want some sort of external medium.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.770
And this comes around to
one more third major benefit

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:03.755
that I've experienced, myself.

00:13:03.755 --> 00:13:05.130
PROFESSOR: Benefit of divination.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:07.360
JAMES LEONARD: Divination
as a cultural practice,

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:09.780
whether it's connected to a
spiritual practice or not.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:14.490
Is how it helps us in a
non-pharmaceutical way

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.740
deal with chronic anxiety.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.230
Like chronic reality based anxiety.

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:22.890
And there are times where I find
myself saying loss is going to happen,

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.840
but it doesn't need to be absolute loss.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:27.174
That even in and of itself
helps people make some sense.

00:13:27.174 --> 00:13:29.340
PROFESSOR: Cause people
think about what that means.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:33.180
JAMES LEONARD: And it helps sort
of either step down expectations

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.070
or get people more on a realistic plane.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:37.980
That, OK, it can't be
business as usual anymore.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.420
But where do we go?

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.190
PROFESSOR: It's been
absolutely fantastic.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.939
And you've opened the
eyes of a scientist.

00:13:42.939 --> 00:13:44.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

